---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Img from '../../components/Img.astro';
import Media from '../../components/Media.astro';
import Video from '../../components/Video.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/BeforeAfter.astro';
---
<CaseLayout
  slug="skate-for-girls"
  eyebrow="UX consulting · WashUX"
  title="SKATE for Girls × WashUX"
  summary="A website redesign for a nonprofit that teaches math through figure skating, built as practical recommendations the client can ship on their existing Wix site."
  hero="/images/skate/final.webp"
  heroAlt="The redesigned SKATE for Girls homepage in purple and aqua"
  snapshot={[
    ['My role', 'Homepage design lead + UX research'],
    ['Team', 'Dhruv Shah, Joselyn Garcia, Dana Sandoval, Jahnavi Patel'],
    ['Client', 'SKATE for Girls (nonprofit)'],
    ['Methods', 'Surveys, user interview, lo-fi to mid-fi'],
  ]}
>
  <section id="overview" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Overview">Overview</h2>
    <p>
      SKATE for Girls (<strong>S</strong>olving <strong>K</strong>inetic <strong>A</strong>nd <strong>T</strong>ransforming
      <strong>E</strong>ducation) is a free program that teaches math to girls in grades 4 to 8 through figure skating and
      movement.
    </p>
    <p>
      We took a consulting approach centered on practical, attainable improvements. Working within the client’s existing
      Wix platform, we looked for ways to improve the experience while making sure every recommendation could realistically
      be implemented.
    </p>
    <div class="callout"><p><strong>The constraint:</strong> built for Wix. Every recommendation had to be something the client could ship themselves.</p></div>
    <p>
      I <strong>led the homepage design</strong>, <strong>initiated the customer discovery interviews</strong>, and
      <strong>curated the user survey</strong> that identified our key areas of focus.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="goals" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Goals">Objectives</h2>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature"><h3>1. Increase donations</h3><p>Make giving easy to find and easy to do.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>2. Emphasize impact</h3><p>Show donors what their contribution turns into.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>3. Clarify the mission</h3><p>Help a first-time visitor understand SFG in seconds.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="research" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Research">Research</h2>
    <p>
      We ran two studies to gauge general impressions of the site, focused on the three pages tied to donor engagement:
      <strong>Home</strong> (first impression), <strong>About</strong> (legitimacy), and <strong>Impact</strong> (what a
      donation actually does).
    </p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>38</b><span>Google Form responses from WashU students</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>45%</b><span>couldn’t find the Donate button at first glance</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>40%</b><span>felt parts of the About page were unnecessary</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>We also held a one-on-one user-testing interview with a 19-year-old former competitive skater and current coach.</p>
    <div class="quotes">
      <blockquote>“It was difficult to figure out what this company was about and what they exactly do.”</blockquote>
      <blockquote>“I see where the button is, but it’s not user-friendly. There’s just a cluster of options.”</blockquote>
      <blockquote>“Do I fill out this form to donate as well?”</blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="findings" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Findings → fixes">From findings to fixes</h2>
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>What we heard</th><th>What we changed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Respondents were overwhelmingly drawn to the acronym and the image on the Home page.</td><td>Replace the ice-surface photo with an image of SFG participants, a more direct call to action.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>45% couldn’t identify the Donate button at a glance.</td><td>Emphasize Donate on the Home page and add a sticky button in the nav so it’s always visible.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>40% felt parts of About were unnecessary, mainly press mentions and long paragraphs.</td><td>Keep every component, condense the mission to what matters most, and move press mentions into a carousel.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Some statistics felt too low or not objective enough.</td><td>Flagged for the client to revisit which statistics lead the Impact page.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="sketches" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Sketches">Low-fidelity sketches</h2>
    <p>
      We sketched lo-fi mockups and iterated on them through collaborative feedback sessions with the client. On the
      Donate page, the second version introduced <strong>tiered giving</strong>: $50 covers snacks and water, $75 covers
      skate rental and ice time, and $100 covers all fees for one student. An abstract gift becomes a concrete outcome.
    </p>
    <Media src="/images/skate/donate-sketches.webp" alt="Two hand-drawn Donate page sketches, the second with $50, $75 and $100 giving tiers, an event ticket card and a merch card" ratio="wide" />
    <p>
      The About page sketches answer the 40% finding directly: a one-sentence mission in large type, an autoplaying mission
      video, and a press slider that animates horizontally as you scroll.
    </p>
    <Media src="/images/skate/about-sketches.webp" alt="Hand-drawn About page sketches with a one-sentence mission, a mission video, and an In the press logo slider" ratio="wide" />
  </section>

  <section id="options" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Options">Exploring options</h2>
    <p>For the Impact page, we tested four placements for testimonials alongside program statistics before picking one.</p>
    <Media src="/images/skate/impact-options.webp" alt="Four grayscale Impact page wireframes with different testimonial placements above Across SFG's Programs statistics" ratio="wide" />
  </section>

  <section id="homepage" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Homepage">The homepage: wireframe to final</h2>
    <p>
      My lead piece. The redesign emphasizes clear storytelling, stronger visual hierarchy, and a better flow: an
      impact statement up top, Move / Skate / Learn pillars, the S.K.A.T.E. acronym, student stories, and Join, Mentor,
      and Donate at the end. Drag to compare.
    </p>
    <BeforeAfter
      before="/images/skate/home-wireframe.webp"
      after="/images/skate/final.webp"
      beforeAlt="Grayscale mid-fidelity homepage wireframe"
      afterAlt="Final purple and aqua homepage design"
    />
    <Video name="skate-prototype" frame="browser" label="Screen recording scrolling through the redesigned SKATE for Girls homepage prototype" caption="The prototype in motion." />
  </section>

  <section id="reflection" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Reflection">Reflection</h2>
    <p>
      The final deliverable was a prototype and a set of actionable recommendations SKATE for Girls can implement
      directly on Wix, built to showcase their mission and impact and to catch the attention of potential donors.
    </p>
    <p>
      This project strengthened my ability to translate user research into strategic design decisions, collaborate with
      stakeholders, and deliver user-centered solutions within real-world constraints.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
